<h2>Optional: a dedicated Tailwater pre-sell micro-site</h2>

<div class="addon">
  <h3>Add a standalone Tailwater micro-site</h3>
  <div class="addon-price">+$4,500 to start. +$237.60 to +$517.60 per month, by tier, only while the micro-site is live.</div>
  <p>If you want Tailwater on its own URL for the parcel signage, ads, and a focused pre-sell funnel that has no builder content competing for attention, this splits Tailwater out of the unified site into its own micro-site. It is built first and launches first, on its own timeline. The Builders site catches up behind it. After the three homes sell, the micro-site winds down and your monthly returns to the tier number you picked above. Long-term, the recurring is the same whether you add this or not. The add-on is for the pre-sell experience, not the long-term economics.</p>
  <p><strong>Pick it if</strong> a memorable URL on the signage at the downtown Hailey park matters to the pre-sell. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you are fine letting the Tailwater section inside the builder site do the pre-sell work, with the builder URL pointing visitors to it.</p>
</div>

<h2>How it rolls out</h2>

<p>The default rollout, without the add-on:</p>

<div class="phase">
  <div class="phase-num">Phase 1</div>
  <h3>The unified site, Tailwater section first</h3>
  <p>I start Marketing Consulting research the day this engagement signs. At the same time I build the unified Raised Bar Builders site, with the Tailwater section completed first so the pre-sell launches while the rest of the builder content is still being finished. The builder content, the ZipKit Homes authorized builder-dealer funnel, and your About page (the heritage, the magazine feature, the General's endorsement) all land in the same launch. The site moves onto Web Management at launch.</p>
</div>

<div class="phase">
  <div class="phase-num">Phase 2</div>
  <h3>F3 Properties takeover</h3>
  <p>I take over F3, fix the broken styling that is degrading the site right now, surface the magazine feature and the General's endorsement properly, and tighten the site inside your monthly pooled hours. F3 joins the management pool as the second site.</p>
</div>

<p>If you take the Tailwater add-on, the order shifts: Phase 1 is the standalone Tailwater micro-site (built and launched first, on its own URL, for the pre-sell), Phase 2 is the Raised Bar Builders site (without the Tailwater section, since it now lives on its own micro-site), Phase 3 is the F3 takeover.</p>

<h2>What each thing actually is</h2>

<h3>The build</h3>
<p>A new website, built as a fixed-fee project, separate from the monthly management. The build covers an agreed set of pages, the design, the forms, the search-engine foundation, and launch. Fifty percent at signing, fifty percent at launch. The build replaces the onboarding fee for that site. The default Raised Bar Builders site, with Tailwater built in, is one Small build at $5,625.</p>

<h3>Web Management</h3>
<p>The monthly that keeps a site running, secure, monitored, updated, and backed up. Every tier includes hosting, daily backups, security monitoring, and uptime monitoring. The tier sets how often software gets updated, how fast I respond when something breaks, and how many hands-on hours are in your monthly pool. Hours are pooled across all the sites I manage for you, so you draw from the same bucket whether the work is on the Builders site or the F3 takeover.</p>

<h3>Marketing Consulting</h3>
<p>Research and strategy applied to your business. I look at your market and your competitors, figure out what people are searching for and where the holes are, and come back with specific recommendations on what to do about it. I never execute on the marketing side, that is the discipline of the product. You give me direction, I come back with strategy. Sold once for the family of companies, not per site or per entity.</p>

<h3>Takeover and onboarding</h3>
<p>F3 is a takeover, not a build. The first month is the takeover audit: who controls the domain, the email, the analytics, the licenses. That month is heavier than every month after it. The $800 onboarding fee on F3 pays for that takeover work. Onboarding ends on a date, the close of month one, not when a task list is empty.</p>

